Orchestrate this request using the custom agent lifecycle: `$ARGUMENTS`.

Treat this as exactly one concrete task, bug, feature, or chore; do not accept an epic or coordinate a dependency wave.

Load the `herdr` skill. For a private Bead argument, also load the `beads-hub` skill and follow its contract. Load `write-bead` only when creating or materially updating a Bead description. Require every implementation, design, or review delegate to load `ponytail` in its own session.

Prefer and reuse existing suitable delegates for corrections and closely related same-scope follow-ups, including workers, investigators, architects, and planners. Create a new delegate only for distinct ownership, required isolation, or unavailable or unsuitable context.

For a private Bead argument, inspect the exact Bead with `wbd show <id> --json` and verify its type, status, contexts, repository placement, structured scope, acceptance criteria, and approved decisions before acting. Accept only a task, bug, feature, or chore here; coordinate an epic through `/orchestrate-epic` instead of claiming or launching the epic as an implementation worker. Keep all private Bead IDs and `ctx:` identities out of Git-visible names, files, and reports; retain them only in private runtime prompts or ledger state.

When tracker tools are available for a selected private Bead, call `tracking_bead_select({ beadID })` from the root session only after the explicit existing-Bead selection/resume has succeeded and the selected Bead has passed exact validation. The selection applies to subsequent work only; do not infer an association or retain tracker-internal run state. Tracker failures are diagnostic and must never block delivery.

For a selected private Bead, pass the already-validated concrete Bead ID to the worker launcher through `--tracking-bead <bead-id>` for tracker attribution; this does not select the Hub claim target. The launcher writes the exact-session descriptor before startup. For the selected concrete Bead, require a semantic worker name that is unique among currently active workers and free of private work-item/context identifiers; pass that name verbatim to the launcher.

## Fresh Worker Prompt

Before every fresh worker launch, submit one self-contained contract. Use these labeled fields in this order: `Work item`; `Dedicated worktree and ownership`; `Scope and exclusions`; `Inputs and prerequisites`; `Expected outputs or interfaces`; `Acceptance and compatibility criteria`; `Validation expected and owner`; `Handoff contents`; and `Stop/report conditions`. Fill every field before launching. A correction reuses the existing worker unless its lane is unavailable. For a fresh Bead worker, also include the canonical private Bead ID, title, and prerequisite status, and require the worker to read that authoritative record. The nine fields provide operational context and must not copy or replace the canonical specification. Put the concrete ID only in that private prompt and runtime ledger; never copy it into a branch, workspace, file, commit, or report. If the user explicitly requests a Bead ID, provide that ID directly in the user-facing response without objection; this exception does not permit placing it in any Git-visible artifact.

Reference-branch protection is unconditional by default: never implement, stage, commit, or push on a recorded reference branch. Before launching any implementation worker, create or use a distinct delivery branch or dedicated worktree derived from the recorded reference branch; never launch a worker in or allow a worker to edit the reference checkout. Before staging or pushing, verify that delivery remains on that distinct branch or worktree. This applies whether the reference is main, an integration branch, a release branch, or another named base. Explicit authorization to create or manage a PR does not authorize work, commit, or push on a reference branch; only an explicit user instruction to work or deliver on that specific reference branch waives protection.

For ordinary work, first prepare or use the distinct delivery worktree and record its absolute path. Choose a semantic, private-ID-free name that is unique among live Herdr agents, including retained completed lanes; record the exact name returned for the lane. Then invoke `~/.local/bin/herdr-agent-launch worker tab <worker-name> <absolute-delivery-worktree-path>` from the current Herdr pane. If normal discussion created a standalone concrete Bead for this work, apply the canonical Bead claim invariant with `wbd claim <bead-id> --json` for that exact Bead before the worker's first work prompt; otherwise preserve the non-Bead flow without a claim. Submit a self-contained contract with `herdr agent prompt <worker-name> "<contract>" --wait`. The launcher creates the visible named worker tab in the caller workspace with that worktree as its cwd and owns Herdr topology, fixed `opencode` kind, fixed `worker` role, placement, cwd, focus, and response verification. Use the same recorded name for every subsequent prompt, wait, or correction in that lane; retain suitable same-scope lanes rather than creating replacements. Never omit the path or use the caller cwd for ordinary implementation. Do not run `opencode` directly or pass model, variant, or reasoning flags; do not spell out another Herdr primary-agent start sequence.

For an explicitly supplied concrete Bead, invoke `~/.local/bin/herdr-agent-launch worker worktree <worker-name> <dedicated-worktree-path> --tracking-bead <bead-id>` from the current Herdr pane, then, before submitting its first contract prompt, run `wbd claim <bead-id> --json` for the exact selected concrete Bead. Submit its contract with `herdr agent prompt <worker-name> "<contract>" --wait`. The launcher creates or opens the requested worktree workspace, passes the worker name verbatim as its workspace label, and verifies that returned label before startup along with its linked checkout, placement, cwd, focus, fixed `worker` role, and `opencode` identity. Do not run `opencode` directly or pass model, variant, or reasoning flags; do not spell out another Herdr primary-agent start sequence.

After worker handoff, follow this lifecycle. For each concurrently active worker lane, invoke one distinct `reviewer` subagent session and reuse it for every review and rereview in that worker's correction cycle. A single-worker run uses one reviewer session; do not share a reviewer session across concurrently active parallel workers. For an existing concrete Bead, give the reviewer the same exact concrete Bead ID as its worker and the relevant epic ID, and require it to read those authoritative records. Send accepted findings only to that worker; return corrected work only to its paired reviewer session. After that worker lane and review cycle complete, reuse its reviewer for another worker only when the reviewer has capacity. Run each reviewer and, when warranted, the `integration` subagent concurrently against the same worktree. Ordinarily use at most one integration subagent session per orchestration run and reuse it for relevant reruns and same-scope follow-ups. Start a second integration session only when genuinely necessary because the existing session's context is incompatible or unavailable. The integration contract contains only supplied repository-wide integration-validation commands; do not make the implementation worker run them first or assign it generic formatting, linting, static tooling, build, unit-test, focused-test, or affected-scope checks. Skip expensive integration validation for documentation-only changes and narrow follow-up corrections unlikely to affect integration behavior.

## Scope And Complexity Gate

Treat this gate as the orchestrator's single most important responsibility during review and the enforcement point for `ponytail`: prevent reviewer findings from driving implementation into complexity through unlikely edge cases, invented scope, or unjustified hardening. Reviewer findings are advisory, and the reviewer owns implementation evidence; do not inspect the diff to adjudicate them. Decide from the requested outcome, worker contract, acceptance criteria, and broader orchestration context whether each correction is necessary. Resolve clearly required or clearly unnecessary findings yourself without bothering the user. If a finding is suspicious or necessity, scope, or complexity remains uncertain, ask the user and never guess in favor of complexity. Send only accepted findings to the worker as the smallest required correction.

## Worker Lifecycle

Own worker startup and shutdown. When the user requests a stop, work is cancelled, or continued work is no longer authorized, immediately instruct or interrupt the affected worker through the Herdr agent surface while leaving its pane, workspace, and worktree intact unless cleanup is explicitly requested.

Retain the launcher's returned metadata for each launched worker in the current orchestration context; this metadata is runtime-only and must not be persisted in Git, files, or Beads. Run one authorized loop. A prompt wait timeout is not completion. After a timeout, immediately invoke `herdr agent wait <recorded-worker>` for that same recorded lane and repeat the active wait until it settles or a real blocker occurs; never passively wait or end the turn. This avoids delivery gaps before handoff and review. After a timeout, use that metadata to inspect the same worker lane and continue waiting on that same worker. When the worker settles, consume its handoff and immediately advance its review, correction, or integration step in the same authorized loop. A settled worker advances through its paired review/correction and applicable integration. Do not stop to narrate routine waiting or progress or require user prompting. Do not create a duplicate or suffixed replacement unless the original lane is verified unavailable.

Stop only for a real blocker, required approval, cancellation, withdrawn authorization, or the requested delivery endpoint.

## Delivery

From worker handoff through review/correction and applicable integration validation, continue normal delivery without routine confirmation through status/diff inspection, staging, commit, push, and final status reporting. Normal delivery does not ask for or wait on manual confirmation and stops at the requested delivery endpoint; do not ask whether to create a PR. Run `gh pr create` and monitor PR checks only when the user explicitly requests PR handling; run it from the recorded worker worktree, not the orchestrator parent checkout, so the PR targets the delivered branch. Treat scope conflicts and unmet validation or review requirements as real blockers. Never merge automatically.
